//! THE FLOOR — the trap seam, the status split, and the whole `@export`ed
//! C-ABI surface (spec §5.7) every typed wrapper above is written on.
//!
//! It is the bottom of the module order because it names no wrapper type: an
//! op here takes a raw handle word and answers a raw status, which is exactly
//! what makes it reachable from every object kind without reaching back into
//! any of them. `checked` is where a number becomes a `Result`, and
//! `sos_handle_release` is the one call every wrapper's `deinit` issues — so
//! the owning tier's whole contract is recorded here too, below every file
//! that implements it.
//!
//! THE TWO SYSCALL EXTERNS LIVE HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, and that is what put
//! the per-op C surface here with them: an `extern "C"` declaration carries no
//! visibility modifier, so it cannot be shared with a sibling module, and two
//! modules declaring one symbol is an ambiguity error at the importer. One
//! declaration, one file, and the typed layer above calls the named seams.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, SystemOp, ProcessOp, ThreadOp, EventOp, WaiterOp,
               InterruptOp, ClockOp, TimerOp, MemoryOp, IoMemoryOp, MappingOp,
               PipeInletOp, PipeOutletOp, PipeReplyOp, PipeRequestOp,
               RELEASE_OP, MINT_OP}

// The per-arch HAL, and since design 172 part 2 the WHOLE of it: the one
// instruction in the system that crosses the trap boundary. FIXED ARITY on
// purpose — a varargs call across a trap boundary has no portable register
// story, so a wider op gets `sos_syscall2` beside this, not an ellipsis.
extern "C" {
    func sos_syscall1(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt) -> UInt
    func sos_syscall3(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt, arg1: UInt, arg2: UInt,
                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) -> UInt
}

// =============================================================================
// Errors
// =============================================================================

// How a syscall reports what happened is `sosabi`'s `SosStatus` (spec §5.7) —
// the SAME declaration the kernel's dispatch returns, so the two halves of the
// contract cannot disagree about what tag 3 means. It carries its own
// `describe()`, `Printable` and `Error` (design 145: an enum takes methods and
// hand-written trait bodies), which is what retired the free `sys_error(status)`
// helper this module used to need. A process reaches all of it through the
// value it is handed and never imports `sosabi` — the type's own module is
// always consulted for its methods (design 142), and an enum case pattern
// resolves from the scrutinee.
//
// So userspace can now interpolate a failure (`print("{}", e)`) as well as
// `match` it. Before, the type could conform to nothing and had to be branched
// on to say anything at all.
//
// IT IS A STATUS, AND ONLY SOME STATUSES ARE ERRORS. `checked` below is the one
// place that distinction is made, and it is what keeps the `Ok` case out of the
// error channel entirely.

/// Turn a raw status word into a Result. The one place a number becomes a type.
///
/// THE SPLIT (ratified Aug 7): `Ok` becomes `Result.Ok`, everything else becomes
/// `Err(status)`. So the `Ok` case exists on the wire, where 0 has to mean
/// something, and cannot exist in an `Err` — `Err(SosStatus.Ok)`, a value that
/// would claim a call both failed and succeeded, is unconstructible downstream
/// because this function is the only constructor of the error channel.
///
/// The narrowing is guarded rather than assumed: `a0` is register-wide, the enum
/// is byte-wide (its width is ABI), and an `as UInt8` on a value above 255 is a
/// PANIC — in a process, over a word the process did not choose. A kernel
/// compiled from this same table never returns one, so the guard is unreachable
/// in M1 and costs a compare; what it buys is that the impossible case is an
/// error value rather than a trap.
public(package) func checked(raw: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    if raw > (UInt8.max as UInt) {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    guard let status = SosStatus.from(raw: raw as UInt8) else {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    if status.is_failure() {
        return status
    }
    return
}

/// The same split for an op that answers with a VALUE — a created thread's
/// handle, a joined thread's exit code, a status word. `Ok` carries the value;
/// anything else is the error and the value is not read.
func checked_value(raw: UInt, value: UInt) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    try checked(raw)
    return value
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE POLL SPLIT — where `WouldBlock` stops being an error (sawos design 13 D-2;
// design 234 §4's two-channel shape)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A WOULD-BLOCK IS NOT AN ERROR AT THE TYPED TIER.** `Channel.try_receive` is
// the precedent design 234 §4 set and the reason it gives is exactly the pipe's:
// a poll loop that cannot tell "nothing yet" from "this is broken" waits for
// ever. So the transient gets the OK channel and a distinguishable absence, and
// only the genuine refusals — a peer that is gone, a full table, a build skew —
// reach the error channel.
//
// TWO FUNCTIONS BECAUSE THE TWO DIRECTIONS ANSWER DIFFERENT SHAPES. A take
// yields a LENGTH, so its absence is an `Optional`; a post yields nothing, so
// its absence is a `Bool` — `Result<Void?, _>` is not a type worth writing, and
// "was it staged" is a question a caller can act on directly. Both keep the
// property that matters: the caller never matches an error case for a condition
// that will clear itself.

/// Decode a status word for an op whose refusal may be transient and whose
/// success carries NO value: `Ok(true)` it happened, `Ok(false)` not right now.
public(package) func polled_ok(raw: UInt) -> Result<Bool, SosStatus> {
    // The same guarded narrowing `checked` writes, and for the same reason: `a0`
    // is register-wide and the enum is byte-wide, so an `as UInt8` on a word a
    // kernel skew produced would be a PANIC in a process over a number it did
    // not choose.
    if raw > (UInt8.max as UInt) {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    guard let status = SosStatus.from(raw: raw as UInt8) else {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    match status {
        case Ok -> { return true },
        case WouldBlock -> { return false },
        case _ -> {},
    }
    return status
}

/// Decode a status word for an op whose refusal may be transient and whose
/// success carries a VALUE: `Ok(Some(v))` here it is, `Ok(None)` not right now.
public(package) func polled_value(raw: UInt,
                                  value: UInt) -> Result<UInt?, SosStatus> {
    if raw > (UInt8.max as UInt) {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    guard let status = SosStatus.from(raw: raw as UInt8) else {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    match status {
        case Ok -> {
            let got: UInt? = value
            return got
        },
        case WouldBlock -> { return None },
        case _ -> {},
    }
    return status
}

/// Issue an op that answers with a value.
///
/// `unsafe` because it names a pointer: the value register comes back through
/// an out-parameter, which is the shape that crosses a C ABI with no aggregate
/// return. The typed methods above call this and stay safe signatures — the
/// pointer never leaves this function, which is design 136's confinement rule.
public(package) func call_value(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt, arg1: UInt,
                                arg2: UInt) unsafe -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_syscall3(handle, op, arg0, arg1, arg2,
                           (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    checked_value(raw, value)
}

// =============================================================================
// The C-ABI surface (spec §5.7) — for non-Saw languages
// =============================================================================
//
// PER-OP functions are the supported C interface: one function per operation,
// named for it, with the arguments the operation actually takes. A C caller
// gets the same contract a Saw caller does minus the type wrapper, and still
// never writes an op number.
//
// `sos_syscall1` underneath is the raw form and is reachable, but it is the
// escape hatch, not the interface: it takes an op number, which is the thing
// this whole arrangement exists to keep out of callers.
//
// Whitelist-clean by construction — handles, ops and statuses are all
// integers, so nothing here needs a type the C ABI cannot carry.

/// Write one byte to the debug console. Requires `SystemRight.Debug`.
/// Returns a status word: 0 on success.
@export("sos_system_debug_print")
public func sos_system_debug_print(handle: UInt, byte: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, SystemOp.DebugPrint as UInt, byte)
}

/// Stop the machine with `status`. Requires `SystemRight.Shutdown`. Does not
/// return on success; returns a status word if the call was refused.
@export("sos_system_shutdown")
public func sos_system_shutdown(handle: UInt, status: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, SystemOp.Shutdown as UInt, status)
}

// The ops that answer with a VALUE take an out-parameter for it, exactly as the
// raw form does. A C caller writes `unsigned long h; sos_process_thread_self(p,
// &h);` and gets the same contract a Saw caller does minus the Result.

/// This process's own handle. Requires `SystemRight.ProcessSelf`.
@export("sos_system_process_self")
public func sos_system_process_self(handle: UInt,
                                    value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ProcessSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Load the image in region `image` into region `memory` and answer with a
/// handle to the new, INERT process. Requires `SystemRight.ProcessCreate` on
/// the caller's SYSTEM handle — processes are minted by the System (sawos
/// design 2's RIDER, ruled Aug 29).
///
/// Both arguments are Memory handles — the read-only source blob and the
/// child's destination RAM. The kernel copies each segment to its link address
/// inside `memory` and grants a stack at that region's top; the caller supplies
/// no addresses at all.
///
/// A malformed image is `SosStatus.BadImage` — a VALUE, not a fault — because
/// image bytes are data the caller was handed rather than something it wrote.
/// A handle that names nothing, or names something that is not a Memory, IS a
/// fault: the caller chose which handles to pass.
@export("sos_system_process_create")
public func sos_system_process_create(handle: UInt, image: UInt, memory: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ProcessCreate as UInt, image, memory, 0,
                 value_out)
}

/// Make a thread in this process, ready to start. Requires
/// `ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`. `entry` may be `ENTRY_IMAGE`.
@export("sos_process_thread_create")
public func sos_process_thread_create(handle: UInt, entry: UInt, stack_top: UInt,
                                      arg: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.ThreadCreate as UInt, entry, stack_top, arg,
                 value_out)
}

/// The CALLING thread's own handle. Requires `ProcessRight.ThreadSelf`.
@export("sos_process_thread_self")
public func sos_process_thread_self(handle: UInt,
                                    value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.ThreadSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End this process with `status`. Requires `ProcessRight.Exit`. Does not
/// return on success.
@export("sos_process_exit")
public func sos_process_exit(handle: UInt, status: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ProcessOp.Exit as UInt, status)
}

/// This process's §8 status word. Requires `ProcessRight.Wait`.
@export("sos_process_get_status")
public func sos_process_get_status(handle: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.GetStatus as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a created thread runnable. Requires `ThreadRight.Start`.
@export("sos_thread_start")
public func sos_thread_start(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Start as UInt, 0)
}

/// Wait for a thread and take its exit value. Requires `ThreadRight.Join`.
@export("sos_thread_join")
public func sos_thread_join(handle: UInt,
                            value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ThreadOp.Join as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End the CALLING thread with `code`. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the
/// caller's own handle. Does not return.
@export("sos_thread_exit")
public func sos_thread_exit(handle: UInt, code: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Exit as UInt, code)
}

/// Give the processor up. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the caller's own
/// handle.
@export("sos_thread_yield")
public func sos_thread_yield(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Yield as UInt, 0)
}

/// Make an Event in this process. Requires `ProcessRight.EventCreate`. `mode`
/// is 0 for bitwise-OR accumulation and 1 for saturating-sum (spec §2.4); any
/// other value terminates the process.
///
/// THE C ALTITUDE WRITES THE MODE AS A NUMBER, and that is the documented
/// difference in kind the module docstring describes rather than an oversight:
/// a C caller has no enum to be handed, so it gets the wire value. The Saw
/// altitude above never writes one — it offers a method per mode.
@export("sos_process_event_create")
public func sos_process_event_create(handle: UInt, mode: UInt,
                                     value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.EventCreate as UInt, mode, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a Waiter in this process. Requires `ProcessRight.WaiterCreate`.
@export("sos_process_waiter_create")
public func sos_process_waiter_create(handle: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.WaiterCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Bind IRQ `line` to a new Interrupt in this process. Requires
/// `ProcessRight.InterruptBind`. A line this machine does not have, the
/// timer's line, or one already bound TERMINATES the process.
@export("sos_process_interrupt_bind")
public func sos_process_interrupt_bind(handle: UInt, line: UInt,
                                         value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.InterruptBind as UInt, line, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End this fire and let the device interrupt again. Requires
/// `InterruptRight.Ack`. Acking one that has not fired terminates the process.
@export("sos_interrupt_ack")
public func sos_interrupt_ack(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, InterruptOp.Ack as UInt, 0)
}

/// Run a created process: mint its first thread at the recorded entry, on the
/// recorded stack, and make it runnable. Requires `ProcessRight.Start` on the
/// CHILD's handle. The caller keeps running.
///
/// A SECOND START TERMINATES THE CALLER — it is broken code, and the caller is
/// the one that started it the first time.
///
/// **`boot_tag_form` SAYS WHETHER `boot_tag` MEANS ANYTHING** (sawos design 4
/// D-4): 0 is the no-tag form and puts nothing (`NO_HANDLE`) in the child's
/// first argument register; 1 resolves `boot_tag` against the child's boot set
/// and puts the CHILD-SIDE word there. Any other value terminates the caller.
///
/// It is a flag argument rather than a reserved tag value because ZERO IS A
/// LEGITIMATE TAG — root's region ordinals start at 0 — and because a tag is the
/// giver's own word, which the kernel must not reserve values out of. A tag that
/// names no given record terminates the caller: it is config the caller wrote
/// both halves of.
///
/// THE C ALTITUDE WRITES THE FORM AS A NUMBER, which is the documented
/// difference in kind the module docstring describes (see
/// `sos_process_event_create`'s mode): a C caller has no enum to be handed. The
/// Saw altitude above never writes one — it offers `start()` and
/// `start(boot_tag:)`.
@export("sos_process_start")
public func sos_process_start(handle: UInt, boot_tag_form: UInt,
                              boot_tag: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.Start as UInt, boot_tag_form, boot_tag, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// **GIVE `given` TO THIS PROCESS UNDER `tag`** — the courier op (sawos design
/// 4). Requires `ProcessRight.Give` on the CHILD's handle — "may place handles
/// into this child's boot set" — and the universal `Transfer` right on the
/// handle BEING GIVEN.
///
/// It MOVES the handle: the caller's entry is unbound and its generation bumped,
/// a fresh entry binds in the child's table carrying the SAME kind, target and
/// RIGHTS, and a record is appended to the child's boot set under `tag`. **THE
/// RETURN IS A STATUS ALONE** — the child-side word is meaningless to the caller,
/// which can call no op through it, so nothing hands it back. What crosses is the
/// TAG, which the child reads at its drain and which the kernel never interprets.
///
/// **THE GIVER'S WORD IS DEAD AFTERWARDS.** Using it again is the ordinary
/// `BadHandle` fault, which is design 3's generations doing the only job they
/// have. Typed Saw callers cannot meet that: `Process.give` consumes the wrapper
/// and disarms it before the syscall.
///
/// FOUR WAYS TO TERMINATE THE CALLER, all caller-checkable: a `given` that names
/// nothing (`BadHandle`); one without `Transfer`, or a receiver handle without
/// `Give` (`AccessDenied`); a child that has already been STARTED — the boot
/// set freezes at start (`BadState`); and a `tag` the child's set already carries
/// (`DuplicateKey`). A full child table is `SosStatus.NoResource`, a STATUS, and
/// the give did not happen — the caller's handle is untouched.
@export("sos_process_give")
public func sos_process_give(handle: UInt, given: UInt, tag: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.Give as UInt, given, tag, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Take the next handle out of this process's BOOT SET, as a record copied into
/// `buf` (spec §12). Requires `ProcessRight.BootHandles`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least
/// `boot_handle_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside
/// this process's writable memory; a buffer that is not TERMINATES the process,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the region ordinal, word 1 a
/// `BootHandleKind`, word 2 the handle. An exhausted set answers
/// `SosStatus.Drained` and keeps answering it — that is a loop's termination
/// check, not a failure.
@export("sos_process_boot_handle_next")
public func sos_process_boot_handle_next(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                         cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.BootHandleNext as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Accumulate `bits` into an Event. Never blocks. Requires `EventRight.Signal`.
@export("sos_event_signal")
public func sos_event_signal(handle: UInt, bits: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, EventOp.Signal as UInt, bits)
}

/// Drain an Event's word and reset it to zero. Requires `EventRight.Receive`.
@export("sos_event_receive")
public func sos_event_receive(handle: UInt,
                              value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, EventOp.Receive as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Attach a waitable to a Waiter under `key`, which must not already be in use
/// on that Waiter. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` on the waiter and
/// `EventRight.Wait` on the target.
///
/// It is the first op taking TWO arguments and answering with a status alone,
/// and the raw forms are fixed-arity by design (§5.7: no varargs across a trap
/// boundary), so it rides the three-argument form and drops the value. The
/// scratch that catches it is what makes this signature `unsafe`; the pointer
/// never leaves the function, which is design 136's confinement rule.
///
/// **AND M4 UNIT 3 SPENT THE THIRD ARGUMENT ON FLAGS** (`designs/010` rulings
/// 9(b) and 11(c)): `WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT` detaches at delivery,
/// `WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING` releases the caller's entry and hands the object to
/// the attachment. The register was already there — this seam was riding the
/// three-argument form with a zero in it — so the growth cost nothing on either
/// side of the trap, which is the §5.7 renumberable-ops discipline paying out a
/// second time. A word carrying any other bit is a `BadArg` fault.
@export("sos_waiter_add")
public func sos_waiter_add(handle: UInt, target: UInt, key: UInt,
                           flags: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, WaiterOp.Add as UInt, target, key, flags,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Detach the attachment registered under `key`. Requires
/// `WaiterRight.Attach`.
///
/// IT TAKES THE KEY, NOT THE WAITABLE'S HANDLE: detaching edits the Waiter's
/// own table and never touches the waitable, so the key is the whole of what
/// names the attachment.
@export("sos_waiter_remove")
public func sos_waiter_remove(handle: UInt, key: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, WaiterOp.Remove as UInt, key)
}

/// Block until an attached handle is ready, and COPY OUT a record describing it
/// into `buf` (spec §2.2, as amended by design 178 unit 3 rider 3). Requires
/// `WaiterRight.Wait`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least
/// `wait_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside this
/// process's writable memory; a buffer that is not TERMINATES the process,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the attacher's key, word 1 a `WaitTag`,
/// word 2 the payload. A C caller reads the words; a Saw caller gets them
/// decoded into a `WaitResult` by `sos.waiter`'s `Waiter.wait`.
@export("sos_waiter_wait")
public func sos_waiter_wait(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                            cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, WaiterOp.Wait as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// This process's Clock for the domain `type` names. Requires
/// `SystemRight.ClockGet`. A `type` that is not a `ClockType` terminates the
/// process.
///
/// **A FRESH HANDLE EVERY TIME onto the one Clock** (sawos design 3 D-4),
/// superseding this seam's old "the same handle every time". Two asks are two
/// capability instances naming one kernel-eternal object; the caller owns each
/// and releases each. A full handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`, which
/// repetition can now reach.
@export("sos_system_clock_get")
public func sos_system_clock_get(handle: UInt, type: UInt,
                                 value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ClockGet as UInt, type, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Read the clock into `buf` — a COPY-OUT record, because a nanosecond count is
/// 64 bits and one profile's registers are not. Requires `ClockRight.Read`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least
/// `clock_now_record_bytes()`. Doubleword 0 is the reading.
@export("sos_clock_now")
public func sos_clock_now(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ClockOp.Now as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Make a Timer bound to this Clock. Requires `ClockRight.TimerCreate`.
@export("sos_clock_timer_create")
public func sos_clock_timer_create(handle: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ClockOp.TimerCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Arm a Timer from a COPY-IN record at `record` — doubleword 0 the delay from
/// now, doubleword 1 the repeat interval (0 for one-shot), both in nanoseconds.
/// Requires `TimerRight.Arm`.
///
/// THIS IS THE COPY-OUT FUNNEL'S MIRROR TWIN, and the first op in the system to
/// hand the kernel a record rather than take one. `bytes` must be EXACTLY
/// `timer_arm_record_bytes()`; a record that is misaligned, outside this
/// process's memory, or the wrong length TERMINATES the process, because all
/// three are mistakes only the caller could have made.
///
/// Arming an already-armed Timer REPLACES its schedule and clears any fires it
/// had not yet delivered. That is not an error — it is how a timeout is
/// changed.
@export("sos_timer_arm")
public func sos_timer_arm(handle: UInt, record: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          bytes: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, TimerOp.Arm as UInt, record as UInt, bytes, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Stop a Timer and discard any fires it had not delivered. Requires
/// `TimerRight.Arm`.
///
/// Disarming one that is not armed is a NO-OP, deliberately — a one-shot
/// disarms itself when it fires, so cancelling a timeout that has just expired
/// is an ordinary race rather than a caller error. (This is the opposite call
/// from `sos_interrupt_ack`, whose no-fire case IS a fault; see `Timer.disarm`.)
@export("sos_timer_disarm")
public func sos_timer_disarm(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, TimerOp.Disarm as UInt, 0)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Memory, IoMemory and Mapping (spec §2.5; sawos design 6)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// FIVE OPS ACROSS THREE KINDS, and the pairing is the design: each memory kind
// has a DERIVATION verb and an INSTALL verb, and the third kind is what an
// install produces. The verbs differ because the kinds do — a split consumes
// what it hands out and a carve does not — which is D-2's whole argument, seen
// from the ABI.

/// **SPLIT — one cut from the FRONT of a region.** Requires
/// `MemoryRight.Split`.
///
/// The new Memory covers the first `len` bytes and THE PARENT BECOMES THE
/// REMAINDER, so repeated splits walk a pool forward and the parent handle is
/// the cursor. `len` must be a non-zero multiple of the target's protection
/// granularity and no larger than the parent; anything else TERMINATES the
/// caller, which chose the number. A full Memory slab or handle table is
/// `SosStatus.NoResource`.
///
/// There is no carve-from-the-middle, and the reason is the representation
/// rather than a policy: a region is one `{base, len}` slot, and a cut from the
/// middle would leave the parent as two ranges.
@export("sos_memory_split")
public func sos_memory_split(handle: UInt, len: UInt,
                             value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, MemoryOp.Split as UInt, len, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// **MAP — install this region in `process`'s address space, and answer with a
/// MAPPING handle.** Requires `MemoryRight.Map` here AND `ProcessRight.Map` on
/// `process`.
///
/// TWO RIGHTS ON TWO OBJECTS, because holding bytes must not imply authority
/// over somebody's address space. `access` is a mask of `MapAccess` bits; write
/// without read, no access at all, and any bit outside the three all TERMINATE
/// the caller. A region whose range this machine cannot grant is the same
/// fault. A domain with no protection row left, or a full Mapping slab or
/// handle table, is `SosStatus.NoResource`.
///
/// THE CALLER SUPPLIES NO ADDRESSES. The range is the region's own, which is
/// why there is still no op that reads a Memory's bounds.
@export("sos_memory_map")
public func sos_memory_map(handle: UInt, process: UInt, access: UInt,
                           value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, MemoryOp.Map as UInt, process, access, 0, value_out)
}

/// **CARVE — a sub-window of this device window, LEAVING THE PARENT WHOLE.**
/// Requires `IoMemoryRight.Carve`.
///
/// `offset` is relative to the parent, so a caller names a sub-window without
/// learning an address. The parent is UNCHANGED — MMIO is pinned and a fixed
/// region may be handed out many times — which is the whole difference from
/// `sos_memory_split`.
///
/// A window this machine cannot express as one protection grant TERMINATES the
/// caller, and the rule is per-profile: one wants a naturally-aligned power of
/// two, the other a whole number of pages. So does a range past the parent's
/// end. A full slab or handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`.
@export("sos_iomemory_carve")
public func sos_iomemory_carve(handle: UInt, offset: UInt, len: UInt,
                               value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, IoMemoryOp.Carve as UInt, offset, len, 0, value_out)
}

/// **MAP — install this device window in `process`'s address space.** Requires
/// `IoMemoryRight.Map` here AND `ProcessRight.Map` on `process`.
///
/// THERE IS NO ACCESS ARGUMENT, and that is the type doing its job: an IoMemory
/// can only ever produce a device-attribute row, read-write, never executable.
/// A driver therefore cannot obtain a cacheable view of a register block by any
/// spelling — the guarantee is the object kind, not a check.
@export("sos_iomemory_map")
public func sos_iomemory_map(handle: UInt, process: UInt,
                             value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, IoMemoryOp.Map as UInt, process, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// **UNMAP — take this mapping's row out of the domain it was installed in.**
/// Requires `MappingRight.Unmap`.
///
/// Only a Mapping unmaps (§2.5), which is what the object is for. A mapping
/// that is already unmapped — or whose target process has died, taking its
/// whole domain with it — TERMINATES the caller with `BadState`: the caller
/// either unmapped it or created the child.
///
/// RELEASING THE HANDLE IS NOT AN UNMAP. Release destroys the entry and never
/// the row (§2.5's stance: a mapping dropped without an unmap is permanent,
/// safe-but-leaked), so a caller that means to revoke says so.
@export("sos_mapping_unmap")
public func sos_mapping_unmap(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, MappingOp.Unmap as UInt, 0)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pair (spec §2.1; sawos design 13)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THREE OPS: make a connection, put a message in, take one out. The factory is
// on the PROCESS object because a pipe is IPC and carries nothing system-shaped
// (design 10 ruling 3), and each END has exactly one op because `post` is the
// one kernel primitive on the client side (ruling 4) and nonblocking `take` is
// its mirror.

/// **MAKE A CONNECTION, and answer with BOTH of its ends** as a record copied
/// into `buf` (spec §2.1). Requires `ProcessRight.PipeCreate`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least
/// `pipe_create_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside this
/// process's writable memory; a buffer that is not TERMINATES the process,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the PipeInlet handle — the client end,
/// where requests flow in — and word 1 the PipeOutlet handle, the server end
/// where they come out. It answers through the funnel rather than the value
/// register for the plainest reason: an op returns ONE word and this one has two
/// to say (`sos_clock_now` is the precedent for answering in memory at all).
///
/// Both ends are minted into the CALLER's table; wiring them outward is
/// `sos_process_give`'s job, which is what makes a launcher able to hand the
/// inlet to one child and keep or pass the outlet. A process at its pipe budget
/// is `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`; a full slab or handle table is
/// `SosStatus.NoResource`.
@export("sos_process_pipe_create")
public func sos_process_pipe_create(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                    cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.PipeCreate as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// **POST — stage `len` bytes from `body` for the far end, and answer with the
/// CLAIM on its reply** through `value_out`. Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`.
///
/// IT NEVER SUSPENDS (design 10 ruling 4). `len` may be 0 — a data-free message
/// is §2.1's own option, and a zero-length post reads no buffer at all, so the
/// address is not even looked at. A `len` above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` TERMINATES the
/// caller: the maximum is published and the caller chose the number. So does a
/// body that is misaligned or outside this process's memory.
///
/// **THE VALUE IS A `PipeReply` HANDLE** (sawos design 14) — the client's claim
/// on the one reply this message is owed, minted into the caller's table. On a
/// refusal nothing is minted and the value means nothing.
///
/// THREE REFUSALS ARE STATUSES: `SosStatus.PeerClosed` when the far end is gone
/// (nobody can ever take it, and it is terminal — nothing un-closes),
/// `SosStatus.WouldBlock` when the ring has no free slot, which is transient and
/// clears when an exchange settles, and `SosStatus.NoResource` /
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded` when there is no room in the caller's table for the
/// claim.
@export("sos_pipe_inlet_post")
public func sos_pipe_inlet_post(handle: UInt, body: UnsafePointer<UInt8>,
                                len: UInt,
                                value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeInletOp.Post as UInt, body as UInt, len, 0,
                 value_out)
}

/// **TAKE — copy the OLDEST staged message into `body`, and answer a RECORD
/// naming its length and the obligation to reply to it.** Requires
/// `PipeOutletRight.Take`.
///
/// IT NEVER PARKS. `rec` is a two-word buffer — word 0 the message's LENGTH,
/// word 1 a `PipeRequest` handle minted into the caller's table — and `rec_cap`
/// is its size in BYTES, which must be at least `pipe_take_record_bytes()`. A
/// smaller one TERMINATES the caller, as does a record buffer outside this
/// process's writable memory.
///
/// `body` takes no capacity of its own: it is checked against `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`,
/// still the published minimum, but against the caller's REAL memory rather than
/// against a number the caller typed — a buffer that short TERMINATES the caller
/// with `BadBuffer`.
///
/// TWO REFUSALS ARE STATUSES: `SosStatus.WouldBlock` when nothing is staged and
/// the far end still exists, and `SosStatus.PeerClosed` when nothing is staged
/// AND the far end is gone. THE ORDER IS THE CONTRACT — everything staged is
/// handed over first, whatever became of the sender, and only a drained ring
/// answers `PeerClosed`. Nothing un-closes, so every later take answers the same.
/// A full table or a spent handle budget is `SosStatus.NoResource` /
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`, with the message left staged for whoever takes it
/// next.
@export("sos_pipe_outlet_take")
public func sos_pipe_outlet_take(handle: UInt, rec: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                 rec_cap: UInt,
                                 body: UnsafePointer<UInt8>) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeOutletOp.Take as UInt, rec as UInt, rec_cap,
                 body as UInt, (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// **RESOLVE — take the reply if it has been written, and answer its length**
/// through `value_out`. Requires `PipeReplyRight.Resolve`.
///
/// IT NEVER PARKS, and unit 2 has no waitability to park with: the blocking
/// `send(msg)` / `send(msg, timeout:)` compositions are unit 3's. `cap` is the
/// buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` — the whole
/// slot's size, because a caller cannot size a buffer against a length it has not
/// been told yet.
///
/// **IT CONSUMES THE CLAIM UNLESS IT ANSWERS `WouldBlock`.** A delivered reply
/// and a `SosStatus.PeerClosed` both end the exchange, so the caller's handle is
/// destroyed with them and using the word again is the ordinary `BadHandle`
/// fault; a would-block consumes nothing and the claim is still yours.
///
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` means the server side abandoned it — the obligation was
/// dropped without a reply, or the connection's server end went away before the
/// message could ever be taken.
@export("sos_pipe_reply_resolve")
public func sos_pipe_reply_resolve(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt8>,
                                   cap: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeReplyOp.Resolve as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 value_out)
}

/// **REPLY — copy `len` bytes from `body` back to the client, and DISCHARGE the
/// obligation.** Requires `PipeRequestRight.Reply`.
///
/// `len` may be 0 — a reply that carries no data is still an answer — and a `len`
/// above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` TERMINATES the caller, exactly as it does at a post.
///
/// **IT ALWAYS CONSUMES THE REQUEST**, which is what single-use means: the
/// caller's handle is destroyed whether the reply landed or not, and using the
/// word again is the ordinary `BadHandle` fault.
///
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` says the reply was not delivered because the client
/// abandoned its claim — §2.1's ratified answer, never a silent success, and
/// ignorable with `let _ =` on the std-Channel close precedent. The obligation is
/// discharged either way.
@export("sos_pipe_request_reply")
public func sos_pipe_request_reply(handle: UInt, body: UnsafePointer<UInt8>,
                                   len: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeRequestOp.Reply as UInt, body as UInt, len, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// **RELEASE — destroy this handle, whatever kind it names** (sawos design 3
/// D-2). The ONE spelling of release in this module.
///
/// It is not `sos_<object>_release` nine times, because release is not an op on
/// any object: it is one universal op number the kernel intercepts before it
/// has decided the kind, so there is one function here for the same reason there
/// is one op number there.
///
/// **UNGATED.** No right is checked. A handle is a capability INSTANCE and the
/// object's identity lives in its slot, so destroying your own instance takes
/// nothing from anybody else holding one. Releasing your System handle or your
/// own Process handle is legal-but-doomed: you lose the authority, which is what
/// you asked for.
///
/// **A WORD THAT DOES NOT RESOLVE TERMINATES THE PROCESS.** `NO_HANDLE`, a
/// malformed word, a word whose handle was already released — all of them are
/// the ordinary `BadHandle` fault, because using a handle you released is broken
/// code and the kernel's generations exist to diagnose it rather than to
/// tolerate it. This function therefore does not return on that path, and its
/// status word is only ever `Ok`.
///
/// TYPED SAW CALLERS DO NOT WRITE THIS. In the typed layer release is DROPPING
/// the wrapper — that is what its `deinit` does, and this is the syscall the
/// deinit issues. It is public because the C altitude is the floor and a non-Saw
/// process needs a way to say it; a Saw process that reaches for it is working
/// with raw words, which is a thing only the raw altitude does.
@export("sos_handle_release")
public func sos_handle_release(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, RELEASE_OP, 0)
}

/// **MINT — a SIBLING handle onto the same object, whatever kind it names** (the
/// Aug-29 user rulings on design 4). The second universal op, beside release and
/// for the same reason: naming an object twice is one act for every kind.
///
/// `keep` is a KEEP MASK: the sibling's rights are the source's INTERSECTED with
/// it, so all-ones means "the same rights" and anything narrower attenuates. The
/// intersection is what makes no-amplification structural — a sibling is a
/// subset of its source by construction — and it is TOTAL, so naming a right the
/// source does not hold is a no-op rather than an error. Its polarity is the
/// ruling: a keep mask FAILS CLOSED when a kind gains a right later, where a
/// removal mask would have passed the new authority through in silence. Write a
/// complement (`~(SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32) & 0xFFFF_FFFF`) when
/// subtractive intent is what you mean — the fail-open choice then reads at the
/// call site.
///
/// **IT IS GATED ON THE UNIVERSAL `Mint` BIT, WHICH IS ITSELF MASKABLE**: a
/// sibling minted through a mask that left `Mint` out cannot mint again, so a
/// launcher stops proliferation exactly where it says it does. A source without
/// `Mint` TERMINATES the caller, as does a word that names nothing.
///
/// The sibling is INDEPENDENT — its own slot, its own generation, its own
/// release — and names the same object, which is what makes it a second NAME
/// rather than a second object.
@export("sos_handle_mint")
public func sos_handle_mint(handle: UInt, keep: UInt,
                            value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, MINT_OP, keep, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// The keep mask that narrows nothing — `System.mint()`'s default.
///
/// It is spelled as every bit rather than as a list of the rights that exist,
/// which is the KEEP mask's own polarity applied to itself: a mask naming the
/// rights of today would silently start narrowing the day a right is added, and
/// "the same rights this handle has" is what the no-argument spelling means.
public static ALL_RIGHTS: UInt32 = 0xFFFF_FFFF

// =============================================================================
// The OWNING tier's contract (sawos design 3 D-5) — what every wrapper's
// `deinit` above this file is written to
// =============================================================================
//
// **EVERY WRAPPER IS `NoCopy`, AND DROP IS RELEASE** (the Aug-29 user ruling).
// Each holds one handle word and one hand-written `deinit` that releases it, so
// a wrapper going out of scope destroys the capability instance it owned —
// RAII, over kernel handles, with the kernel's own generations backstopping it.
//
// There were nine when the ruling landed and there are ELEVEN since M3 unit 4
// (`IoMemory`, `Mapping`); the count is not written here any more, because the
// rule is what matters and a number in prose is what goes stale. `Mapping` is
// the one worth reading its own docstring for: dropping it releases the HANDLE
// and does not unmap the ROW, which is §2.5's ruled stance rather than an
// omission.
//
// WHY THE TWO HALVES HAD TO LAND TOGETHER. A release op beside COPYABLE wrappers
// is a stale-fault factory: copy a `Clock`, let one copy drop, and the sibling's
// next use is a `BadHandle` fault the program did nothing to deserve. `NoCopy`
// is what makes "this value owns that word" true, and it is what §3's
// no-DUPLICATE rule always meant at this altitude.
//
// **THE DISARM SENTINEL IS THE WORD FIELD ITSELF**, which costs no second field
// and cannot get out of step with one: `NO_HANDLE` is unrepresentable as a LIVE
// handle (design 3 D-1 makes it the index byte's zero at every generation), so
// a field holding it is exactly a wrapper that has already given its word away.
//
// **THERE IS NO TYPED `release()` METHOD, deliberately.** Early release is
// dropping the value — `let _ = move clock` — which is one concept for the
// reader instead of two ways to say it, and it is the same spelling every other
// owning type in Saw uses. A `release()` beside a RAII deinit would also be a
// second way to reach the disarmed state, which is precisely the state the
// sentinel exists to make unreachable from user code.
//
// **THE TRANSFER-FUNNEL CONTRACT, recorded now for unit 3's `give` and M4's
// pipes.** When a kernel op MOVES the word out of the caller's table, the sysapi
// funnel that owns that syscall CONSUMES the wrapper, reads the word, sets the
// field to `NO_HANDLE` BEFORE issuing the syscall, and lets the disarmed value
// drop. Disarm-before-syscall is the deliberate order: every failure of such a
// syscall is a FAULT (the process ends, and teardown covers everything it held),
// so there is no path on which a disarmed-but-unsent word leaks. User code never
// touches the sentinel. Generations are the backstop: a funnel bug that released
// after a transfer is a diagnosed `BadHandle` fault, never corruption.
//
// **`shutdown()` NEVER RETURNS**, so wrappers live at that moment never run
// their deinit and die in the kernel's teardown instead. That is the backstop
// working as designed rather than a leak, and the transcript's `handles=` count
// is what says which path each handle took.

// =============================================================================
// The boot handle the runtime hooks print through (design 172 part 2)
// =============================================================================
//
// It sits at the floor because TWO files need it and neither is below the
// other: `sos.system` WRITES it, out of `System(boot_handle:)`, and `sos.rt`
// READS it, out of the panic path's writer. A helper two seams share lives
// below both.

/// The boot handle, parked for the runtime hooks in `sos.rt`.
///
/// The runtime seams take no handle — the ABI has no room for one — so the one
/// root is given at boot is remembered here by `System.init` before anything
/// can print. It is root's own authority being kept in root's own address
/// space, NOT ambient authority: a process that was never given the handle has
/// nothing to remember and its panics are simply silent.
///
/// `unsafe static var` (design 149) rather than an `Atomic`: it is plain
/// compound process state written once at boot, and spec §7's uniprocessor
/// argument covers it exactly as it covers the arena.
public(package) unsafe static var SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 0

/// Park the boot handle. A small named `unsafe` helper is how design 136 says
/// to confine contact with an unsafe-typed value, and it is what keeps the
/// public `System(boot_handle:)` an ordinary safe signature.
public(package) func park_system_handle(handle: UInt) unsafe {
    SYSTEM_HANDLE = handle
}
